# A long request's admission without Python-list round trips. Rank 0 sent a request's whole prompt to rank 1 in its
# ADMIT, and both ranks walked it as Python objects several times on their scheduler threads, where every stream's
# decoding waits: a 1,000,000-token ADMIT kept rank 0 for 323 ms and rank 1 for 136 ms (x86 workstation, measured; a
# GB10 core is slower). Now the request thread makes the prompt's int32 array in short pieces (submit), the ADMIT sends
# it as one part of the message, the message is sealed as one int64 array with a CRC-32 checksum in place of the
# position-weighted sum, rank 0 reads nothing back from its own all-gather, and rank 1 takes the message as an array and
# each payload in one C pass: 7 ms on rank 0 and 35 ms on rank 1 for the same ADMIT (steady state, x86). The values on
# the wire are unchanged but for the checksum, which both ranks of one build compute alike. mgllm, 2026-10-08.
# Tests: tests/test_9009_lean_admit.py (CPU).

